WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.044
Frontend TypeScript can make browser code more organized, but it does not automatically make rendering safe.

2
00:00:07.044 --> 00:00:13.483
Framework escaping, safe templating, and predictable component patterns are important defenses.

3
00:00:13.483 --> 00:00:21.587
Developers should be careful with APIs that insert raw HTML, manually manipulate the DOM, or build URLs from untrusted values.

4
00:00:21.587 --> 00:00:26.587
If a framework provides safe rendering conventions, use them intentionally.

5
00:00:26.587 --> 00:00:28.708
Browser storage also needs care.

6
00:00:28.708 --> 00:00:36.964
Values placed in local storage, session storage, IndexedDB, or other browser-accessible locations can be read by browser code.

7
00:00:36.964 --> 00:00:40.297
Sensitive data should not be stored there casually.

8
00:00:40.297 --> 00:00:54.006
Cookies, tokens, session state, and framework authentication helpers need to be understood at a high level so teams know which values are accessible to browser code and which are protected by server-side controls.

9
00:00:54.006 --> 00:00:59.081
Client-side checks can improve usability, but they are not final enforcement.

10
00:00:59.081 --> 00:01:06.656
Route guards, hidden buttons, disabled controls, and client-side role checks can guide a user through the interface.

11
00:01:06.656 --> 00:01:11.958
They should not be the only thing protecting backend data or sensitive operations.

12
00:01:11.958 --> 00:01:19.002
The server must enforce authorization because server-side code is the trusted place to make those decisions.

13
00:01:19.002 --> 00:01:21.881
Other browser security settings matter too.

14
00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:32.561
Content security policy, CSRF protections, secure cookie options, same-site behavior, and framework defaults should be reviewed in the context of the application.

15
00:01:32.561 --> 00:01:44.756
The practical habit is to let TypeScript clarify frontend state while relying on secure rendering, framework-aware configuration, and trusted server enforcement for security decisions.

16
00:01:44.756 --> 00:01:50.513
Frontend code should also avoid placing sensitive assumptions in scattered components.

17
00:01:50.513 --> 00:01:59.602
A shared helper for safe URL creation, a small wrapper for trusted rendering patterns, or documented storage rules can reduce inconsistency.

18
00:01:59.602 --> 00:02:08.767
The safer pattern is to make the secure path easy for ordinary feature work, not something every component has to rediscover during each change.
